# `plugins`

```ts
plugins: Bundle[];
```

A plugin brings directives: the components that render them and the handlers that reshape what is written inside them. A package exports a function that returns one, and you call it:

```js
import { steps } from '@acme/steps';

mdsvex({ plugins: [steps()] });
```

````svx score
:::steps

## Install

```sh
pnpm add mdsvex
```

## Configure

Add it to the config.
:::
````

Here each heading inside `:::steps` starts a step. The plugin wraps the heading and what follows it in a `step` directive with the heading as its label, so the page renders the plugin's `steps` and `step` components.

The first-party plugins are listed under [Plugins](/docs/plugins).

::::plate[The plugin object](mark="{}", tone=expr)
A plugin is an object:

```js
export function steps() {
	return {
		name: 'steps',
		// exports a component for each directive below, under its name
		module: '@acme/steps/directives',
		directives: {
			steps: {
				children: {
					heading(node, ctx) {
						ctx.state.step?.close();
						ctx.state.step = node.wrap_from('directive_container', { name: 'step' });
						node.wrap_from('directive_label').close();
					}
				}
			},
			step: {}
		}
	};
}
```

| key | what it is |
| --- | --- |
| `name` | names the plugin in errors and in the editor manifest |
| `module` | the module that exports the directive components. It resolves as an import from the Vite root would |
| `directives` | one entry per directive name the plugin owns |
| `parse` | a [parse plugin](/docs/plugins/writing/parse-plugins#parse_plugins) for the whole document, the same object an entry of `parse_plugins` is |
| `components` | element replacements, as `{ specifier, names }` |
| `fences` | the fence languages the plugin renders itself, such as `['mermaid']`, which the highlighter never sees |

:::notes
`compile()` takes `plugins` as well. It writes `module` as the import specifier exactly as given.
:::
::::

::::plate[Handlers bound to a directive](mark=":::", tone=directive)
An entry of `directives` can hold handlers. They are bound to that directive, so they never run anywhere else in the document:

| key | called for |
| --- | --- |
| `children` | nodes written directly inside a container directive of this name, by node type |
| `descendants` | nodes at any depth inside it, by node type |
| `parse` | the directive itself, in any of its three forms |

:::notes
- **Your own export wins.** A `directives` export of the same name from a [`components` module](/docs/guides/directives#declaring-directives) or a template replaces the plugin's component. The plugin's handlers still run, so your `step` gets the same `label` and `children`.
- **Other plugins still see the nodes.** A heading inside `:::steps` reaches the `heading` handlers of `parse_plugins` too, after the bound handler ran. A plugin's `parse` runs ahead of `parse_plugins`.
:::

:::edges
- **A nested blockquote or list is not a child.** `children` means written directly inside the directive. Use `descendants` for every node of a type at any depth.
- **Only containers have children.** The bracket text of a leaf or inline directive is not passed to `children` or `descendants` handlers. Their own `parse` still runs, though an inline directive gets its args after it, so read them in the function `parse` returns.
- **A directive has one owner.** Two plugins with an entry for the same directive throw a `DirectiveOwnerError` when the config loads, naming both. Another plugin reads the directive by observing it, below.
:::
::::

::::plate[The handler's context](mark="ctx", tone=expr, id=handler-context)
Each handler gets the node and a context:

- `ctx.directive` is the directive it is bound to.
- `ctx.index` counts the calls of this handler in this directive, from 0.
- `ctx.state` is an empty object, new for each directive in the document. Keep what a handler needs between calls there, such as the step it opened last.
- `ctx.scratch` is the plugin's, one for the whole document and shared with its other handlers and its document hook, see [plugin handlers](/docs/plugins/writing/parse-plugins#plugin-handlers).
::::

::::plate[A handler can return a function](mark="=>", tone=expr)
A handler can return a function, which runs when the node closes. The directive's own `parse` can read its args when it opens and write them when it closes:

```js
tabs: {
	parse(tabs, ctx) {
		return () => {
			tabs.attrs.args = { ...tabs.attrs.args, count: String(ctx.state.count ?? 0) };
		};
	},
	children: {
		heading(node, ctx) {
			ctx.state.count = ctx.index + 1;
		}
	}
}
```

:::notes
Args are always strings, since they reach the component as string props.
:::
::::

::::plate[Observing a directive](mark="observe", tone=directive)
A plugin that wants to read a directive another plugin owns observes it, with `observe` in its `parse`, or in an entry of `parse_plugins`. This numbers the figures of a figure plugin and leaves what it finds as [plugin data](/docs/plugins/writing/plugin-data), which the figure's component can read:

```js
export function numbering() {
	return {
		name: 'numbering',
		parse: {
			observe: {
				figure: {
					parse(figure, ctx) {
						const number = (ctx.scratch.figures = (ctx.scratch.figures ?? 0) + 1);
						const id = figure.attrs.args?.id;
						if (id === undefined) return;
						return () => {
							const label = figure.first_child;
							ctx.set('target:' + id, {
								number,
								caption: label?.type === 'directive_label' ? label.text_content : null
							});
						};
					}
				}
			}
		}
	};
}
```

:::notes
- **It takes the same entries** as a plugin's own `directives`: `parse`, `children` and `descendants`, with a `ctx.state` and `ctx.index` of its own.
- **It runs after the owner,** at the open and at the close, so it sees the args and the children as the owner left them.
- **It runs whoever renders the directive:** a plugin, your own component, or nothing yet.
:::

:::edges
- **It changes no node.** It writes data, warns, and reads the tree. Writing to a node, the directive or anything else, throws a `TypeError` that names the plugin that owns the directive.
:::
::::

::::plate[Claiming a fence language](mark="```", tone=fence)
A plugin that renders the fences of a language itself claims them with `fences`. `@mdsvex/plugin-diagram` draws each `mermaid` fence and keeps its code as the diagram's source:

```js
export function diagram() {
	return {
		name: 'diagram',
		module: '@acme/diagram/directives',
		directives: { diagram: {} },
		fences: ['mermaid'],
		parse: {
			code_fence: {
				parse(node) {
					if (fence_meta(node.info).lang !== 'mermaid') return;
					node.wrap_from('directive_container', { name: 'diagram' }).close();
				}
			}
		}
	};
}
```

:::notes
- **The highlighter never sees a claimed fence.** It renders as plain code, as with `highlight: false`, and there is no `unknown_language` warning for it. That holds for twinkleplop and for a highlighter function, and saves the work of highlighting code the plugin replaces. It still goes to a replaced `pre`, with its `lang`, `title` and the rest.
- **The editor knows.** The manifest lists the claimed languages, so the language server does not warn of them either.
:::

:::edges
- **A language has one owner.** Two plugins that claim the same language throw a `FenceOwnerError` when the config loads, with `language` and both plugin names as `plugins`.
- **Only fences.** A code span with a hint, `` `#!mermaid A --> B` ``, is highlighted as before.
- **The language as written**, the first word of the info string, so `Mermaid` is not `mermaid`. Claim what your handler matches.
- **`math` fences** are rendered by the [`math`](/docs/reference/options#math) option before any highlighter, so claiming `math` changes nothing while it is on.
:::
::::

What a handler gets and can do is under [plugin handlers](/docs/plugins/writing/parse-plugins#plugin-handlers).

